§ `VerifyDeadBranchElsewhereFixture`

-- Under a NOT, a condition the context entails is, read the other way round,
-- an alternative that cannot hold where it sits: the normal form's dead
-- branch, which is reported alone (verify-written-guards.l4, (11) and (12)).
-- Here a dead branch somewhere else in the rule means the same as such a
-- condition, read the other way round, but is not it. Each condition is
-- reported, as a redundant condition.

GIVEN x IS A BOOLEAN, y IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `either` x y IF x OR y

-- The requirement's `(a OR b)`, which `b` entails, is redundant. The scope's
-- `NOT (either a b)` is a dead branch that means `NOT (a OR b)`, in the
-- other part of the rule.
GIVEN a IS A BOOLEAN, b IS A BOOLEAN, c IS A BOOLEAN, p IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the dead branch is in the scope` a b c p IF
    (a AND (p OR NOT (`either` a b))) IMPLIES (c AND NOT (b AND (a OR b)))

-- The control: the same requirement, and no dead branch in the scope.
GIVEN a IS A BOOLEAN, b IS A BOOLEAN, c IS A BOOLEAN, p IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `no dead branch in the scope` a b c p IF
    (a AND p) IMPLIES (c AND NOT (b AND (a OR b)))

-- The same with no seam. The dead branch is the call, where the NOT the
-- condition stands under writes out `a OR b`.
GIVEN a IS A BOOLEAN, b IS A BOOLEAN, c IS A BOOLEAN, p IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the dead branch is a call` a b c p IF
    (c AND NOT (b AND (a OR b))) AND (q OR (a AND (p OR NOT (`either` a b))))
  WHERE
    q MEANS FALSE

-- The scope's second `a`, which the first entails, is redundant. The
-- requirement's second `a` is too, and it is the requirement's own dead
-- branch, `NOT a`, read the other way round, so it is reported as that alone.
GIVEN a IS A BOOLEAN, b IS A BOOLEAN, c IS A BOOLEAN, d IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the dead branch is in the requirement` a b c d IF
    ((a AND NOT (a AND b)) OR c) IMPLIES (a AND NOT (a AND d))

-- The control the other way: the call under the NOT, which `a` entails, is
-- the dead branch, read the other way round, and is reported as that alone.
GIVEN a IS A BOOLEAN, b IS A BOOLEAN, c IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the dead branch is the call under the NOT` a b c IF
    a AND NOT ((`either` a b) AND c)

-- Two NOTs cancel, so a condition under two is a conjunct again. The `a` of
-- `a AND b`, under one NOT, is the dead branch `NOT a`, read the other way
-- round, and is reported as that alone. The `a` of `a AND c`, under two, is
-- redundant beside the first `a`, as that one is beside it, and both are
-- reported: the dead branch means the negation of each, but is neither.
GIVEN a IS A BOOLEAN, b IS A BOOLEAN, c IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a condition under two NOTs` a b c IF
    a AND (NOT (a AND b)) AND (NOT (NOT (a AND c)))
